    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2006008432617279717",
      "url": "https://x.com/CountofCoins/status/2006008432617279717",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/CountofCoins/status/2006008432617279717",
      "text": "✖️The Manila Trench Megarisk is the single greatest point of failure for our global society.🌏\n\n2 weeks ago I wrote about the ability of small disasters to leverage catastrophic economic consequences. \n\nToday, I have identified the most devastating disaster the world would ever reasonably face - and the simulation results are shocking 🌊\n\n>50-200 day breakdowns at critical ports = semiconductors & cheap consumer goods collapse\n\n>disruption to the Straits of Malacca + South China sea = oil to East Asia is halved & global commodity pricing limbo\n\n>20 million refugees = huge HADR and geopolitical exploitation in South China sea and beyond\n\n>deep-sea cables cut = internet blackouts and continental disconnection\n\n>shattering the Golden Triangle of tech = ending Asia's influence in global export\n\nThese are only a snippet of what happens when a 9.0 thrust event hits.\n\nHere's the science 🌊\n\nThe Philippines sits at the intersection of three tectonic plates (the Philippine Sea Plate, the Eurasian Plate, and the Sunda Plate) and atop the Pacific Ring of Fire.\n\nThe Manila trench, located west of Luzon, is a subduction zone where the Eurasian Plate is sliding under the Philippines. It has been seismically quiet for centuries, meaning it is currently 'locked' and accumulating massive strain.\n\nA rupture would trigger a 'mega-tsunami' with waves exceeding 10 meters (33 feet) and would affect the entire Asia-Pacific - changing the seafloor, flooding coasts, and creating long-term wetlands in dry areas.\n\nFurther break down of the 4 major risks 🌊\n\nBecause the Manila trench sits at the nexus of the 'Silicon Inputs' (Taiwan), Asia's 'Factory Delta' (Pearl River Delta), Asia's 'Gas Intersection' (Straits of Malacca + South China Seas), and Asia's 'Undersea Cable Crossroads,' the GDP and strategic losses would be structural rather than just cyclical.\n\n>Silicon Inputs:\n\nKaohsiung/Tainan is home to critical advanced semiconductor nodes. \n\nEven if 'clean rooms' remain watertight, the vibration from a 9.0 earthquake event would misalign lithography machines worth $200M each = a 6-month freeze in advanced chip supply --> a $2.5 Trillion drag on global GDP as automotive, AI, and consumer electronics industries.\n\n>Asia's Factory Delta\n\nThe Pearl River Delta (GBA) accounts for nearly 10% of China’s total GDP. \n\nA 3-meter surge in the flat delta region from this tsunami would push salt water miles inland & seawater destroys heavy machinery and electrical grids permanently.\n\nRecent simulations suggest that a Manila Trench event would close the ports of Hong Kong and Shenzhen for up to 200 days, extinguishing $3.4 Billion in trade value per day from the global system.\n\n>Asia's Gas Intersection\n\nA global supply chain fracture would develop as a result of this megathrust. A Manila Trench tsunami would shut down the Straits of Malacca and much of the South China sea = the price of oil and LNG will diverge wildly between the Atlantic and Pacific.\n\nIt is the most vital energy artery on the planet.\n\nThe Straits are shallow and sudden shifts in the seabed and massive sediment displacement would render existing nautical charts obsolete. But VLCCs cannot navigate without precise depth data. A single grounded tanker would block the entire world's energy supply for weeks.\n\nThe Straits alone pass roughly 1/3 of all global seaborne oil trade.\n\nJapan & South Korea get nearly 90% of their petroleum and 60% of their LNG from this corridor. Without this flow, their economies have approximately 90 days of strategic reserves before a total industrial halt = mega inflation (especially for the JPY).\n\nRoughly 60% to 70% of China's oil imports come through this 'gas intersection.' The country's economy can be strangled at this single chokepoint.\n\nAnd the geopolitical angle makes it even crazier.\n\nThe South China sea is littered with oil and gas platforms (specifically off the coasts of Vietnam, Malaysia, and Brunei).\n\nTsunami waves traveling through deep water have immense kinetic energy. They would sheer the legs of older platforms and snap undersea pipelines = a massive, multi-national environmental disaster that would require 'Disaster Sovereignty' to manage --> giving China a pretext to 'police' the entire sea for environmental reasons.\n\nChina could use it's vast SPR as a diplomatic carrot for ASEAN (while hedging local demand via Russian pipelines) --> offering oil to Philippines/Vietnam in exchange for dropping South China sea claims.\n\nLikewise, other powers with high reserves (like the US) will have immense leverage over energy-starved nations.\n\nSatellite internet and massive carrier-lead naval fleets become critical to maintaining civilization in these areas. Two areas the US dominate in.\n\nTo sum up the most probable geopolitical 🌏 outcomes:\n\n>Oil prices likely spike to $200+ per barrel within 48 hours.\n\n>Japan, South Korea (and the greater ASEAN) may be forced to negotiate directly with Beijing for energy security if the US cannot provide an immediate energy bridge.\n\n>Physical changes to the South China Sea floor will be used by all parties to re-interpret maritime borders and Exclusive Economic Zones.\n\n>US strategic communications advantage (space-based internet/comms) + energy dominance + refugee support (through Carrier Groups) = huge push for US to contest China.\n\nMore on these second order geopolitical outcomes in another thread.\n\n>Undersea Cable Crossroads\n\nThe Manila Trench is a graveyard for undersea fiber-optic cables. A megathrust event would likely trigger submarine landslides, severing the cables that link the US to Singapore and Southeast Asia, forcing all regional data onto high-latency satellite backups.\n\nThe effects on economies, cultures, and political relationships could be profound.\n\nThis topic is too vast for one tweet, so expect more on this in future updates.\n\nTo sum up the economic risk 👇\n\nEstimated Global Economic Costs:\n\nTotal Regional Direct Loss: ~$500 Billion+\n\nGlobal Supply Chain Multiplier: ~$3 Trillion+ (24mos)\n\nA Manila trench 9.0 megathrust would likely lead to the insolvency of several regional re-insurers, triggering a secondary liquidity crisis in Asian markets.\n\nNet assessment:\n\nA Manila trench disaster would act as the most influential environmental event in modern human history.\n\nHopefully, these scenarios will remain fiction, and we will never have to trade these markets or assess the real humanitarian impact.\n\nSee you on the margins.✖️5",
